{"data":[{"id":"10.5281/zenodo.20558869","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.5281/zenodo.20558869","identifiers":[{"identifier":"oai:zenodo.org:20558869","identifierType":"oai"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Claudio","familyName":"Govi","name":"Govi, Claudio","nameIdentifiers":[{"nameIdentifierScheme":"ORCID","nameIdentifier":"0009-0005-9020-0691"}],"affiliation":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Deterministic prime window explorer beyond the 64-bit barrier  using the GC-60 sieve wheel method with three parallel binaries (M30x3)."}],"publisher":"Zenodo","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"prime numbers"},{"subject":"sieve"},{"subject":"number theory"},{"subject":"deterministic"},{"subject":"beyond 64-bit"},{"subject":"GC60"},{"subject":"wheel"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-05","dateType":"Issued"},{"date":"2026-06-05","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":"it","types":{"schemaOrg":"ScholarlyArticle","resourceTypeGeneral":"Text","citeproc":"article-journal","bibtex":"article","ris":"RPRT","resourceType":""},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.5281/zenodo.20558868","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"V1.0.0","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"},{"rightsUri":"http://rightsstatements.org/vocab/InC/1.0/","rights":"Govi Claudio"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Description: GC60-M30x3-suffix is a computational tool for deterministic prime number discovery within arbitrary windows at magnitudes beyond the 64-bit barrier, based on the GC-60 sieve wheel method.\n\nThe program uses three parallel binaries (M30x3) covering disjoint residue classes modulo 30, allowing each thread to work on a separate portion of the candidate space with no overlap and no redundancy. The sieve phase traverses all primes up to the square root of the target base without exception, guaranteeing deterministic and complete results.\n\nBenchmarks were produced on an AMD Ryzen 7 4800U mini PC (8 cores, 16 threads, 32 GB RAM). Verified results include prime windows at magnitudes 10²⁴ through 10²⁷, with execution times ranging from 203 seconds to approximately 3.5 hours."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://zenodo.org/doi/10.5281/zenodo.20558869","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"api","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-05T14:48:39Z","registered":"2026-06-05T14:48:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-15T09:02:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"cern.zenodo","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.15873","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2608.15873","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2608.15873","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Murali","familyName":"Menon","name":"Menon, Murali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Two Questions on $G$-harmonic Tuples"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Group Theory (math.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"20D60, 20D05, 20B30","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-08-16T17:49:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-18T01:02:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"An $n$-tuple of positive integers is $G$-harmonic if there are subgroups of $G$ having those indices whose cosets can be chosen pairwise disjoint, and $\\mathbb{Z}$-harmonic if there are pairwise disjoint residue classes with those moduli. Ginosar asked whether every $G$-harmonic tuple is $\\mathbb{Z}$-harmonic. Margolis and Schnabel proved this for tuples of length at most $4$, and analysed a particular family of length-$5$ tuples that would yield a counterexample if any member were $G$-harmonic. We show that the bound $4$ is sharp: $(6,6,6,10,15)$ is $A_5$-harmonic but not $\\mathbb{Z}$-harmonic. Moreover, the five pairwise disjoint cosets realising this tuple can be extended to a coset partition of $A_5$ using only cosets of indices $6$, $10$, and $15$. The index tuple of this partition is not $\\mathbb{Z}$-harmonic; because its indices repeat, this does not contradict the Herzog--Schönheim conjecture. We also prove that no member of the length-$5$ family analysed by Margolis and Schnabel in connection with possible counterexamples is $G$-harmonic for any group $G$."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.15873","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"cita